/** \addtogroup area_queue */
/** \defgroup theory_queue Theory of queues
 \ingroup area_queue

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/queue` realize the QUEUE of section
 4.3.1 of the book, with a circular vector (section 4.3.3) and with a singly
 linked list (exercise 4.10), the DEQUEUE of exercise 4.9, a double ended
 queue on a circular vector that doubles, a queue with no duplicates, and two
 algorithms that use a queue through its operations alone. This page
 collects the definitions and the notation that the headers share (see
 \ref theory_queue_notation), the realizations with the costs of their
 operations (see \ref theory_queue_repr), the arithmetic of the circular
 vectors (see \ref theory_queue_circular), and the queues with no duplicates
 and the algorithms on a queue (see \ref theory_queue_algo). The pseudocode
 of each operation is on the page of its header, in the comment of its
 function, together with the section of the book that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_queue_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>queue</em> is a sequence of items to which items are added at one end,
 the <em>back</em>, and from which they are removed at the other, the
 <em>front</em>: the first item added is the first removed (FIFO, first in
 first out). The QUEUE of the book has `enqueue()`, which adds an item at the
 back, `dequeue()`, which removes the item at the front and returns it,
 `top()`, which reads it, and `empty()`; its axioms are
 \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) = \mathrm{top}( Q ) \f$ and
 \f$ \mathrm{dequeue}( \mathrm{enqueue}( Q , v ) ) =
 \mathrm{enqueue}( \mathrm{dequeue}( Q ) , v ) \f$ for \f$ Q \f$ not empty
 (see \ref theory_ADS_adt, and the concept `Queue` of `ADT.h`). A queue is
 what a computation needs when it serves its requests in the order of their
 arrival, e.g., the visit in breadth of a graph, which examines the nodes by
 increasing distance from the root because they leave the queue in the order
 in which they were reached. A <em>double ended queue</em>, or deque, is
 changed at both ends, and is a stack when used at one end only and a queue
 when items enter at one end and leave at the other.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$</td><td>the number of items of a queue</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ m \f$</td><td>the number of places of a circular
     vector, its capacity</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$</td><td>the place of the front in a circular
     vector</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ A[ 0 .. m - 1 ] \f$</td><td>a circular vector, the place 0
     following the place \f$ m - 1 \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_queue_repr Realizations and their costs

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the QUEUE and of the DEQUEUE and the cost of
          their operations (section 4.3.3 of the book, exercises 4.9 and
          4.10)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Representation</th><th>Space</th>
     <th>Operations at the ends</th><th>Capacity</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArrayQueue</tt></td><td>a circular vector</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( m ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ m \f$, fixed</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>LinkedQueue</tt></td><td>a singly linked list with pointers to
     its first and last cells</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>none</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Dequeue</tt></td><td>a bidirectional list with a
     sentinel</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ at both
     ends</td><td>none</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RingDeque</tt></td><td>a circular vector of \f$ 2^j \f$ places
     that doubles</td><td>fewer than \f$ 2 n + 8 \f$ places</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized at both ends, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ by
     rank</td><td>none</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>UniqueQueue</tt></td><td>a bidirectional list, with no
     duplicates</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$ to enqueue, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ to dequeue</td>
     <td>none</td></tr>
 </table>

 A singly linked list is a queue when the items enter after its last cell and
 leave from its first one, since the removal of the first cell and the
 insertion after the last one need no predecessor (see Lemma 1 of
 \ref theory_sequence); the list keeps a pointer to each of the two
 cells, and an empty list has both nil. The deque needs instead the removal
 of the last cell too, i.e., its predecessor, which a bidirectional list has
 at hand. Among the vectors, a queue whose items stayed in the places from 0
 on would move all of them at each `dequeue()`, i.e., \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$; a
 circular vector lets the front move instead, as follows.

 \section theory_queue_circular Circular vectors

 In a circular vector \f$ A[ 0 .. m - 1 ] \f$ the place 0 follows the place
 \f$ m - 1 \f$, and a queue of \f$ n \le m \f$ items with its front at the
 place \f$ h \f$ holds the item of rank \f$ i \f$ from the front, for
 \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$, at the place
 \f[
   ( h + i ) \bmod m , \tag{1}
 \f]
 so that its back is at \f$ ( h + n - 1 ) \bmod m \f$ and the next item enters
 at \f$ ( h + n ) \bmod m \f$. A `dequeue()` advances \f$ h \f$ by one place
 and an `enqueue()` writes after the back, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$; the items
 run through the vector as a band that wraps around its end, and the
 vector is full when \f$ n = m \f$, which a vector with the two indices of the
 front and of the back alone could not tell from the empty one (both have
 the indices equal), whence the count \f$ n \f$ kept beside \f$ h \f$. Since
 \f$ h < m \f$ and \f$ n \le m \f$, the sum \f$ h + n \f$ is below
 \f$ 2 m \f$, and the modulo of (1) is a comparison and a subtraction rather
 than a division.

 `RingDeque` takes \f$ m = 2^j \f$, so that the modulo is the mask
 \f$ m - 1 \f$, and keeps the front and the back as counters modulo
 \f$ 2^{64} \f$ whose difference is \f$ n \f$: an insertion at the back and a
 removal from the front change one counter each, and the place of a counter
 \f$ c \f$ is \f$ c \mathbin{\&} ( m - 1 ) \f$. When the vector is full it
 doubles, and its items are moved in order to the front of the new vector,
 in at most two contiguous pieces, \f$ A[ h .. m ) \f$ and the part that
 wrapped around; by the argument of the vectors that double (see
 \ref theory_sequence_amortized), \f$ n \f$ insertions move fewer than
 \f$ 2 n \f$ items. An insertion at the front of a full deque puts the new
 item at the last place of the new vector, and the front there, which (1)
 makes the place before the place 0. Unlike a deque that only grows at its
 back, the places freed at one end serve the insertions at the other, so
 that a deque used as a queue in a steady state never moves its items.

 \section theory_queue_algo Queues with no duplicates and algorithms on a queue

 `UniqueQueue` refuses the duplicates by one of two policies: `IGNORE_NEW`
 does not enqueue an item already in the queue, so that each item keeps the
 place of its first arrival, as the visits of a graph want it, and
 `FORGET_OLD` removes the old copy and enqueues the item at the back, so that
 it keeps the place of its last arrival, as a cache of the least recently
 used items wants it. Either way `enqueue()` must find the copy, which on a
 list costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ comparisons; the invariant is that no item occurs
 twice, which requires an equality that is an equivalence (an item unequal
 to itself, e.g., a NaN, would be enqueued again and again).

 The functions of `QueueAlgorithms.h` act on a queue through its operations
 alone. Moving the positive items of a queue \f$ q \f$ to another one in
 their order dequeues each item of \f$ q \f$ once, \f$ O( n ) \f$; copying
 them instead, with \f$ q \f$ left as it was, enqueues each item in an
 auxiliary queue as it is dequeued and moves the auxiliary queue back,
 which, by the order FIFO of both, restores the order of \f$ q \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ time and space. A queue cannot be restored by rotating it
 alone, i.e., by enqueueing each item again in \f$ q \f$ as it is dequeued,
 without knowing how many items it holds, which the QUEUE of the book does
 not say.

 \section theory_queue_refs References

 \see A. Montresor, Algoritmi e Strutture Dati, 2025, sections 4.3.1 and
      4.3.3, exercises 4.9 and 4.10,
      https://algoritmicamente.it/materiali/libro/capitoli/cap04.pdf
 \see D. E. Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, vol. 1, 3rd ed.,
      Addison-Wesley, 1997, sections 2.2.1 (stacks, queues and deques) and
      2.2.2 (the circular queue)
 \see U. Pape, Implementation and efficiency of Moore-algorithms for the
      shortest route problem, Mathematical Programming 7(1), 212-222, 1974,
      doi:10.1007/BF01585517
*/
